        "modelAnswer": "One primary cause of fierce opposition was the <strong>judicial breakthrough of Brown v. Board of Education (1954)</strong>. Led by Chief Justice Earl Warren, the Supreme Court unanimously overturned <em>Plessy</em>, ruling that segregated schools were 'inherently unequal' and unconstitutional. Southern whites viewed the decision as a tyrannical federal assault on states' rights and traditional Southern racial hierarchy. However, the subsequent 1955 'Brown II' ruling vaguely ordered desegregation 'with all deliberate speed', inviting Southern authorities to obstruct integration. Therefore, this caused massive resistance because Southern whites resisted federal intervention while exploiting vague judicial timelines to delay compliance.<br><br>Furthermore, opposition was mobilised by the <strong>rapid growth of middle-class White Citizens' Councils</strong>. Founded in Mississippi in 1954, these councils reached 250,000 members, using economic intimidation rather than Klan violence. Councils pressured banks to deny mortgages and forced employers to fire Black parents who attempted to enroll children in white schools, alongside boycotting white moderates. Therefore, this led to effective delays in desegregation because economic retaliation terrorised Black families into withdrawing desegregation petitions.<br><br>In addition, opposition was entrenched by <strong>political 'Massive Resistance' orchestrated by Southern governors</strong>. In 1956, 101 Southern congressmen signed the Southern Manifesto pledging to resist desegregation. In 1957, Governor Orval Faubus deployed the Arkansas National Guard to block the 'Little Rock Nine' from Central High School. Therefore, this forced intense political confrontation because state governors used state troops and legislative obstruction to directly defy federal integration orders.",
