        "modelAnswer": "One major catalyst for the movement's growth was the <strong>inspirational triumph of the Montgomery Bus Boycott (1955–56)</strong>. Sparked by Rosa Parks' arrest, the Black community maintained a 381-day boycott under the Montgomery Improvement Association led by Dr Martin Luther King Jr. Relying on carpools, activists inflicted heavy losses on the bus company until the Supreme Court ruled bus segregation unconstitutional in <em>Browder v. Gayle</em>. Therefore, this caused the movement to grow rapidly because it proved disciplined, non-violent mass collective action could defeat segregation and catapulted King into a nationally respected civil rights leader.<br><br>Furthermore, momentum was generated by the <strong>symbolic passage of federal civil rights legislation</strong>. In 1957 and 1960, the Eisenhower administration passed the first federal civil rights acts since Reconstruction. Although filibusters diluted enforcement, the 1957 Act established the Civil Rights Commission to monitor voter suppression. Therefore, this led to organizational expansion because federal legislative recognition demonstrated to activists that grassroots campaigning could successfully compel Washington to intervene in Southern racial affairs.<br><br>In addition, the movement expanded through <strong>youth direct action and the establishment of SNCC in 1960</strong>. In February 1960, four students staged a sit-in at a segregated Woolworth's lunch counter in Greensboro, North Carolina. Within weeks, the tactic spread to 55 cities, drawing 70,000 participants and leading to the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee (SNCC). Therefore, this accelerated movement growth because youth direct action expanded protests into nationwide student campaigns that forced commercial integration.",
