            "text": "One reason opposition delayed progress was the <strong>Southern Manifesto of 1956</strong>. Signed by 101 Southern congressmen and senators, including Strom Thurmond, the document attacked the Supreme Court's <em>Brown</em> decision as an abuse of judicial power. It encouraged Southern state legislatures to enact laws preventing desegregation and defying federal authority.<br><br>Another reason was violent terrorism by the <strong>Ku Klux Klan (KKK)</strong>. The Klan reformed into violent klaverns, carrying out bombings, cross burnings, and murders of civil rights activists, such as the murder of Emmett Till in 1955. Their violence terrified Black communities and made them afraid to challenge segregation.<br><br>In conclusion, the Southern Manifesto and Klan terror successfully delayed civil rights progress.",
