        "marks": 4,
        "targetMins": 5,
        "questionText": "Study Interpretations 1 and 2. They give different views about opposition to the civil rights movement in the late 1950s. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations.",
        "stimulus": [],
        "sourceExtract": "<div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Interpretation 1:</strong> From a modern historical study.</div><div>Opposition to civil rights in the late 1950s was highly organized and politically entrenched, successfully stalling desegregation. By signing the Southern Manifesto, Southern congressmen created a unified political front that legitimized defiance. At the local level, White Citizens Councils waged economic warfare, firing Black workers and denying them loans. This combination of political resistance and economic terror effectively delayed integration for years.</div></div><hr style=\"border:none; border-top:1px dashed #cbd5e1; margin:12px 0;\"><div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Interpretation 2:</strong> From a different historical perspective.</div><div>Although Southern opposition was loud and politically unified on paper, it ultimately failed to halt civil rights progress. The extreme nature of the opposition's resistance and local violence actually backfired. It shocked Northern public opinion and forced a reluctant federal government to intervene, culminating in the passage of the Civil Rights Acts of 1957 and 1960. These laws established the vital precedent of federal voting rights protection.</div></div>",
        "modelAnswer": "The main difference is that <strong>Interpretation 1</strong> argues late-1950s opposition was \"highly organized and politically entrenched, successfully stalling desegregation\" for years through the Southern Manifesto and White Citizens’ Councils. In contrast, <strong>Interpretation 2</strong> argues Southern opposition \"ultimately failed to halt civil rights progress\" because its extreme resistance \"actually backfired\", shocking Northern opinion and prompting the Civil Rights Acts of 1957 and 1960.",
        "pdfUrl": "/pdfs/mocks/usa/usa_mock_12_question_booklet.pdf",
        "mockUrl": "/units/usa/usa_mock_12_question_booklet.html"
