        "sourceExtract": "<div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\">Source A: From a report by a journalist who witnessed the civil rights demonstrations in Birmingham, Alabama, May 1963.</div><div>We saw hundreds of young schoolchildren marching peacefully down the street. Police Commissioner Bull Connor ordered his men to turn high-pressure fire hoses on them. The water was so powerful it knocked them off their feet and ripped their clothes. Then, they released police dogs that bit several children. The sounds of screaming children and barking dogs filled the air, all captured on television cameras.</div>",
        "modelAnswer": "<strong>Sample Acceptable Inferences (the response of the police to civil rights demonstrators in Birmingham in May 1963):</strong>\n• <strong>Inference 1:</strong> Police authorities used extreme, violent force against peaceful child demonstrators.<br>&bull; <em>Detail from source:</em> \"Police Commissioner Bull Connor ordered his men to turn high-pressure fire hoses on them. The water was so powerful it knocked them off their feet and ripped their clothes.\"\n\n• <strong>Inference 2:</strong> The violent police retaliation was exposed publicly to a national audience via the media.<br>&bull; <em>Detail from source:</em> \"The sounds of screaming children and barking dogs filled the air, all captured on television cameras.\"",
