        "stimulus": [],
        "sourceExtract": "<div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Interpretation 1:</strong> From a modern historical study.</div><div>The decisions made by the Kennedy administration in 1961 marked a key phase in the escalation of US involvement. The deployment of advisers grew from several hundred to over nine thousand by 1962, alongside a major expansion of military aid. Kennedy also authorized tactical operations involving defoliants, establishing a pattern of direct military support.</div></div><hr style=\"border:none; border-top:1px dashed #cbd5e1; margin:12px 0;\"><div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Interpretation 2:</strong> From a different historical perspective.</div><div>President Johnson was primarily responsible for converting the advisory mission into an active combat war. Following the Gulf of Tonkin incident, he initiated air strikes against North Vietnam and approved plans for a sustained bombing campaign. His deployment of two combat divisions in early 1965 initiated the direct involvement of US ground forces in active fighting.</div></div>",
