        "type": "8-Mark Source Utility",
        "marks": 8,
        "targetMins": 12,
        "questionText": "How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into religious opposition to the Nazi regime in the years 1933–39? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your knowledge of the historical context.",
        "stimulus": [],
        "sourceExtract": "<div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Source B:</strong> From a secret Gestapo report on religious attitudes in Upper Bavaria, July 1937.</div><div>\"We must report that the influence of the Catholic Church on the rural population remains unbroken. The Nazi spirit cannot penetrate these districts because the local population is entirely under the influence of the priests. These people prefer to believe what the priests say from the pulpit rather than the words of our best party speakers. When our officials attempted to remove crucifixes from the local schoolrooms, women gathered in protest, and we had to restore them to prevent civil unrest. The Concordat prevents us from making direct arrests of these priests during church services, which they exploit to spread subtle criticisms of the government's youth policies.\"</div></div><hr style=\"border:none; border-top:1px dashed #cbd5e1; margin:12px 0;\"><div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Source C:</strong> From a sermon preached by Pastor Martin Niemöller in Berlin, June 1937.</div><div>\"We have no more desire than our fathers to stand in judgment over our rulers, but we must obey God rather than men. Our brother pastors are being arrested because they spoke the truth. The government's Reich Church seeks to replace the Cross of Christ with the Swastika, and to force our children into youth organisations that teach them to worship a man. We are told that if we remain silent, the Church will have peace. But I say to you, a silent Church is a dead Church. We must speak out, even if it leads us to the concentration camps.\"</div></div>",
        "modelAnswer": "Source B is highly useful for evaluating the strength and typicality of Catholic resistance in rural Germany. The content reveals that the Catholic Church maintained a powerful hold over rural populations, noting that \"the Nazi spirit cannot penetrate\" because people believed \"priests... rather than the words of our best party speakers\". It is particularly useful for demonstrating the limits of Nazi control, showing that when the state attempted to enforce <em>Gleichschaltung</em> by removing crucifixes, popular protest forced them to back down. The provenance—a secret Gestapo report from Upper Bavaria in July 1937—increases its usefulness because it represents an objective, internal assessment of Nazi failures, free from public propaganda. It proves that the Gestapo felt restricted by the Concordat, which prevented them from making direct arrests during services. Contextually, we know that Hitler signed the Concordat with the Pope in July 1933 to secure Catholic neutrality, but systematically broke it, making this 1937 Gestapo frustration highly representative of the ongoing religious friction.<br><br>Source C is extremely useful for investigating the motivations and nature of Protestant opposition. The content shows that pastors like Niemöller opposed the state-controlled Reich Church, accusing it of trying to \"replace the Cross of Christ with the Swastika\" and forcing children into Nazified youth organisations. It is useful because it shows the moral framework of the resistance, stating they \"must obey God rather than men\". The provenance—a sermon preached by Martin Niemöller in June 1937—is highly useful because Niemöller was the co-founder of the Confessing Church, which represented over 6,000 Protestant pastors resisting Nazification. The sermon shows that leaders were fully aware of the personal risks, acknowledging that speaking out would lead to \"the concentration camps\". Contextually, we know that Niemöller was indeed arrested by the Gestapo shortly after this sermon in July 1937 and imprisoned in Sachsenhausen and Dachau, validating the source's depiction of the state's ruthless suppression of religious dissent.",
        "pdfUrl": "/pdfs/mocks/weimar_nazi_germany/mock_notebook_2_question_booklet.pdf",
        "mockUrl": "/units/weimar_nazi_germany/mock_notebook_2_question_booklet.html"
