        "modelAnswer": "One major driver of change was the <strong>emancipation of women during the Weimar Republic</strong>. Under Article 109 of the Weimar Constitution, women enjoyed equal civic rights, voting rights, and equal pay in public service. The 'New Woman' saw 100,000 female teachers and 3,000 doctors by the late 1920s, alongside one million in clerical roles, experiencing unheralded economic independence. Therefore, this caused a major shift in women's status because democratic constitutional protections enabled women to enter the formal workforce and challenge traditional domestic roles.<br><br>Furthermore, women's lives were transformed by <strong>Nazi pro-natalist policies after 1933</strong>. Believing women's sole purpose was 'Children, Kitchen, Church' (<em>Kinder, Küche, Kirche</em>), the Nazi regime purged female civil servants, doctors, and judges. In 1933, the Marriage Law offered interest-free loans of 1,000 marks, with 25% written off per child born, alongside the Mother's Cross rewarding large families. Therefore, this led to a severe regression in women's social position because the totalitarian state subordinated female autonomy entirely to domestic childbearing.<br><br>In addition, policies shifted dramatically due to <strong>wartime labour shortages under the Four-Year Plan after 1937</strong>. As rapid rearmament drained the male workforce, the regime reversed its stay-at-home dogma, introducing compulsory labour service for unmarried women in 1938 and recruiting over 1.5 million women into munitions and agriculture. Therefore, this forced another fundamental change in women's status because urgent war preparations compelled the regime to abandon its domestic rhetoric in favour of industrial female mobilization.",
