        "modelAnswer": "One way in which surgery was different was the introduction of effective <strong>anaesthetics</strong> and <strong>antiseptic methods</strong> to eliminate pain and infection. In the early nineteenth century, surgery was performed without anaesthetics on fully conscious, agonizing patients held down by strong assistants, forcing surgeons like Robert Liston to amputate limbs in under thirty seconds to prevent fatal shock, with over 45% dying of gangrene. In contrast, by the late nineteenth century, surgeons used chloroform to render patients completely unconscious and applied Joseph Lister's carbolic acid and aseptic autoclaves to sterilize operating theatres, allowing complex internal procedures lasting hours to take place safely. Therefore, while early nineteenth-century surgery was rushed, agonising, and deadly from shock and sepsis, late nineteenth-century surgery was pain-free, meticulous, and performed in sterile environments with high survival rates.",
