        "sourceExtract": "",
        "modelAnswer": "It is widely argued that the development of anaesthetics was the most important surgical breakthrough of the nineteenth century, as conquering excruciating physical pain transformed surgical practice from a terrifying ordeal into a viable medical specialty. However, while anaesthetics solved the immediate crisis of pain and fatal shock, they initially increased patient mortality during the 'Black Period' of surgery. Therefore, I disagree that anaesthetics were the single most important breakthrough, because without Joseph Lister's discovery of antiseptics and the subsequent transition to aseptic surgery, anaesthetics alone resulted in more infections and deaths rather than saving lives.<br><br>On the one hand, there is strong evidence that anaesthetics represented a monumental breakthrough. Before the 1840s, surgical operations were severely constrained by agony; patients had to be forcibly strapped to tables while surgeons performed rapid amputations in seconds to prevent the patient dying of cardiac shock or blood loss. In 1847, Edinburgh obstetrician <strong>James Simpson</strong> discovered the anaesthetic properties of <strong>chloroform</strong>, which rendered patients completely unconscious, relaxed muscle tissue, and permitted surgeons to operate with far greater care and precision. The breakthrough gained national and royal legitimacy in 1853 when Queen Victoria publicly praised chloroform during the birth of Prince Leopold. By eliminating agony, anaesthetics made patients willing to undergo life-saving operations that they previously would have refused, creating the foundation for all modern surgical intervention.<br><br>On the other hand, the development of anaesthetics alone had unintended, catastrophic consequences known as the <strong>'Black Period' of surgery (1846–1865)</strong>. Because patients were unconscious and felt no pain, ambitious surgeons attempted longer, deeper, and far more invasive abdominal and internal procedures. However, because surgeons had no understanding of germs or sterile technique, they continued operating in blood-stained frock coats using dirty, unwashed hands and infected sponges. Consequently, rates of hospital gangrene, sepsis, and pyemia soared, and overall surgical mortality actually increased in the two decades following the introduction of anaesthetics. This proves that anaesthetics alone were insufficient and dangerously incomplete without a solution to infection.<br><br>Therefore, the discovery of <strong>antiseptics by Joseph Lister</strong> and the transition to <strong>aseptic surgery</strong> was fundamentally more important. Inspired by Louis Pasteur's 1861 Germ Theory, Lister realized in 1865 that surgical sepsis was caused by living airborne microbes entering open wounds. By soaking wound dressings in carbolic acid, spraying carbolic mist in operating theatres, and sterilizing catgut ligatures, Lister reduced surgical mortality at Glasgow Royal Infirmary from 45.7% to 15.0%. By the 1880s and 1890s, surgeons perfected Lister's breakthrough into complete asepsis: using Charles Chamberland's steam autoclaves to sterilize instruments at high heat, scrubbing hands, and wearing sterile rubber gloves and gowns. This completely eliminated microscopic pathogens from the operating theatre, making deep abdominal, chest, and brain surgery genuinely safe for the first time in history.<br><br>In conclusion, although the development of anaesthetics was indispensable in conquering pain and psychological trauma, it cannot be considered the most important breakthrough. Pain relief without infection control merely led to longer operations and higher mortality during the Black Period. The decisive turning point that allowed surgery to become truly lifesaving was Lister's antiseptic technique and aseptic operating theatres, because controlling bacterial infection was the factor that directly determined whether a patient lived or died.",
